landing-review: model=claude-opus-5 x2 (independent, parallel, read-only) effort=medium range=main..03cb2bd date=2026-09-19
plus a third read-only verification pass over the fix itself (main..9bfe20d), which is where
half of what follows comes from.

both reviewers independently reached the same HIGH, and it is the larger half of this landing.
unpinning `restored` made a stand-down reachable that had been dead code, and it does not stand
down for a frame -- it stands down for the session. `restore_want` latches `s_rjTried` at five
sites (tagpu_vk_unit.c:1276,1316,1319,1337,1352), cleared only by teardown, and its own first
guard `if (s_rjTried) { s_arHave = 0; return; }` at :1301 then forces the twin absent, so one
Vulkan resource refusal drew NO UNIT AT ALL for the rest of the session. the routine case is
worse because nothing has to fail for it: `rlistRepaint` has exactly one assignment
(tagpu_gaf.c:129) and both call sites of `rlist_restart` (:208, :584) pass 0, so it is a
constant 0 and `if (!repaint) s_arHave = 0;` fires on every generation change -- every level
boundary, atlas recycle and map change blanking units, features and effects. this broke a
contract `atlas_rgb_build`'s own header had already written down: a device that will not give
us the RGBA8 "loses restored frames rather than the pass".

fix: a missing twin clears `h.restored` (uRestored goes 0, the shader takes the palette path)
in all three passes and in the aniso gate. a bound on what the flag may promise, not a timing
mitigation -- the hazard here is intra-thread ordering, not a race, so a fence would have fixed
nothing while reading as though it had.

THE FIRST CUT OF THAT FIX WAS WRONG AND THE VERIFICATION PASS CAUGHT IT. it cleared the flag on
the `!feed` branch alone -- the rare half. a generation change satisfies every feed term
(`restoreFrames` non-NULL, `s_arImg` built, `s_rjTried` still 0), so the routine case took
`feed = 1` and left through `feedout` still drawing nothing, while the note asserted it was
fixed. the flag is now cleared on BOTH branches, the `feedout` label is gone, and the feed frame
draws indexed; `restore_want` takes no command buffer and never reads `restored`, so the job is
still made and nothing it recorded can conflict with a draw.

the descriptor had to follow and the fix would have been incomplete without it. binding 43 named
the twin on `s_arView` alone; `mk_image` creates that image UNDEFINED (tagpu_vk_unit.c:491) and
only the restorer's job transitions it, so a frame drawing with the image made but unpainted
declared SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL over an UNDEFINED image -- survivable only while the removed
stand-down kept those frames from drawing at all. it now tests `s_arView && s_arHave`
(:2633-2634), the same pair that decides uRestored, so flag and descriptor agree by construction
rather than by a refusal placed elsewhere. tagpu_vk_terr.c:1080 settled this for binding 42 in
the 11-5c re-review; this is that shape adopted, not reinvented.

two regressions the first cut introduced, both found by the same pass and both fixed: it killed
the `s_saidAniso` latch (`goto standdown` had made the reset unreachable while the condition
held, so a fall-through logs the line EVERY frame), and it left two messages saying "nothing
drawn" and "the other lane" on paths that now draw and about a lane that does not exist. a third
such message was found later, in the aniso gate itself, and fixed in 2e026c5.

the reviews' other findings, each verified against the source before acting: tagpu_posedraw.c
asked `tagpu_classicpp_on()` where feat, fx and terr ask `tagpu_classicpp_assets()`, so turning
assets off mid-session left units on the twin while everything else reverted -- mixed art,
silently, until restart; the hand-over now clears `restored` alongside the list it promises, so
"flag set, list absent" is unrepresentable rather than merely unreached; tagpu_gui_surf.c:643,
the ONE caller of tagpu_gaf_atlas_free_buffers, still said that call does not free rlist; and
`rgbAniso` REVERSED 11-5e-2's review prescription -- `s_twinAniso` is the knob clamped by the
device and is 0.0f wherever the extension is absent, so publishing the constant that review
asked for would have drawn no units at all there. the consumer compares against the knob
(`s_twinAnisoWant`) instead, and `want > 1.0f` (:863) is a third term the first write-up had
left out: at the documented A/B setting aniso=1 the applied value is 0.0f on EVERY device.

also discharged here: the obligation 11-5e-2b's review wrote into this entry. `rlist_add`'s
header denied a `free` this same landing added at tagpu_gaf.c:638, in three places in the
shipped code. what is true is narrower and is now stated as such, with the residual named --
the free does not bound a consumer that captured the pointer, and that is unreachable today only
because free_buffers has exactly one call site, the GUI atlas, which never arms a list.

MEASURED, TWO FIXTURES, because neither does both halves.
crowd-static (16 unit types atlasing after the arm) is the log oracle: main paints the 25 frames
the arm seeded and never another -- on main there is no append at all -- and the branch paints
158, identically in two independent runs. zero VK errors, zero bound restarts.
one-unit is the pixel oracle, four interleaved arms on the committed tree: 302 px cross-build,
identical on all four pairings -- 44 in the minimap, 258 in a box DERIVED as the union of the
four bounding boxes (x 502..516 y 355..388, the placed unit), and 0 px outside both. same-build
control 48 px, all minimap. census 749 452 non-black px on every arm. the branch-only "drawing
the indexed atlas" line appears exactly once per branch arm and never on main.
crowd-static FAILS gate 1 by ~12 000 px (idle COB scripts), one-unit is a live skirmish whose
minimap drifts -- so the fixture is picked per ORACLE, not per landing. and one grab in twelve
of the first run was an outlier (40 556 px over 99 tiles while the grabs either side agreed to
0 px inside that whole region), which is why the method takes three and reads gate 1 on the
settled pair. both lessons are in the ta-drive skill.

